## Roll a deployment back

A deployment of the service (a new image or task definition, from
`cdk deploy`) is a rolling one: ECS starts new tasks beside the old ones,
sends them traffic once they pass the load balancer's health check on
`/health`, and only then stops the old ones, so the service never runs fewer
than its two tasks. Two things fail a deployment and return the service to
the last one that completed, by themselves:

- The circuit breaker, when the new tasks don't start or keep failing the
  health check: three failures in a row (half the desired count, at least
  three).
- The deployment alarms, when the new tasks start but answer badly:
  `LoadBalancer-5xx-Rate-Critical` and `Service-5xx-Rate-Critical` (over
  5 % of the answers 5xx, three minutes in a row; a stray 5xx only sets off
  the `-5xx-Warning` alarms, which roll nothing back) and
  `Service-Unhealthy-Tasks-Critical` (a task failing its health check in two
  minutes of three). ECS watches them while the deployment runs and for a
  bake time after it (under five minutes by default), which is why a
  deployment takes that much longer to complete.

Either way CloudFormation sees the service fail to stabilize and rolls the
whole stack back (`UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE`). `aws ecs describe-services
--cluster <cluster> --services <service> --query 'services[0].deployments'`
says which deployment is running, its `rolloutState` and why it failed
(`rolloutStateReason`); the service's events say the same.

- If the stack stops in `UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED`, fix what it names and run
  `aws cloudformation continue-update-rollback --stack-name {{ .Container }}`.
- A deploy that succeeded but misbehaves later: deploy the last good commit.
  Check out its tag or commit, run `make check`, then
  `cdk deploy -c tier=production --parameters CertificateArn=<arn>`. The
  service, the load balancer and the alarms go back; the data does not. If
  the bad version wrote bad data, restore the items as above to the moment
  it was deployed.
- Never deploy a change that replaces the table (a new key, a new name): the
  deploy makes a new, empty table and keeps the old one outside the stack,
  its data unreachable by the service. Moving data to a new table is a
  migration, planned and run by a person; if it happened by mistake, deploy
  the last good commit and bring the old table back as in
  [The table is gone](#the-table-is-gone).
- Termination protection refuses `cdk destroy` in production. Turning it
  off is the account owner's decision; the table, the vault and the log
  bucket stay anyway.

If any of the three deployment alarms is firing when a deployment starts,
ECS watches none of them for that deployment: only the circuit breaker can
roll it back. That lets a deploy that fixes the incident go out. For any
other deploy, first let the firing alarm clear, or, once its cause is gone,
set it back by hand
(`aws cloudwatch set-alarm-state --alarm-name <name> --state-value OK --state-reason "<why>"`),
so the deployment is watched; and know that a deploy started while one
fires rolls back only if its tasks fail to start or to pass the health
check.

## Alarms

Every alarm notifies the stack's alarm topic (the output `AlarmTopicArn`);
the `AlertEmail` parameter subscribes an address, which must confirm the
email AWS sends it. The dashboard `{{ .Container }}` shows each alarm's metric. Each
alarm fires on one bad five-minute period and clears itself when the next
one is good, but the three a deployment rolls back on, which read one-minute
periods: the two `-5xx-Rate-` alarms fire after three bad minutes in a row,
`Service-Unhealthy-Tasks-Critical` after two of three. An alarm's name is the
stack's, `{{ .Container }}-`, then the name below. The load balancer reports nothing
while no requests come, which its alarms count as good; Container Insights
reporting no running tasks counts as too few.

| Alarm                                             | Means                                                                                                                                                                    | First                                                                                                                                                        |
| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `LoadBalancer-5xx-Rate-Critical`                  | over 5 % of the requests got a 5xx from the load balancer itself, three minutes in a row: no healthy task to send them to (503), or none answered (502) or in time (504) | look at the running and unhealthy tasks on the dashboard, then the app's logs for an error at start-up; a deployment that just went out rolls back by itself |
| `LoadBalancer-5xx-Warning`                        | the load balancer answered a 5xx itself, once or more in five minutes                                                                                                    | if it comes back, as for `LoadBalancer-5xx-Rate-Critical`; a lone 502 can be a task stopping mid-request                                                     |
| `Service-5xx-Rate-Critical`                       | over 5 % of the app's answers were 5xx, three minutes in a row: requests fail, most often at the table                                                                   | read the app's logs for `The request failed.` and the request id; check the table's alarms; a deployment that just went out rolls back by itself             |
| `Service-5xx-Warning`                             | the app answered a 5xx, once or more in five minutes                                                                                                                     | read the app's logs for `The request failed.` and the request id: each one is a bug or a failing dependency                                                  |
| `Service-Latency-P99-Warning`                     | the slowest answers take over a second                                                                                                                                   | check the table's latency and the tasks' CPU on the dashboard; a slow request's trace in X-Ray shows where the time went                                     |
| `Service-Unhealthy-Tasks-Critical`                | a task failed the health check on `/health`, in two minutes of three                                                                                                     | read that task's logs (stream `app/App/<task id>`); ECS replaces it, and a deployment rolls back                                                             |
| `Service-CPU-Usage-Warning`                       | the tasks use over 90 % of their CPU: scaling is at its four tasks, or can't keep up                                                                                     | check the request count; raise the most tasks or the task's CPU in `infra/service_stack.ts`                                                                  |
| `Service-Memory-Usage-Warning`                    | the tasks use over 90 % of their memory: a leak, or a task too small                                                                                                     | watch whether memory only grows between deploys (a leak); otherwise raise the task's memory                                                                  |
| `Service-Running-Tasks-Critical`                  | fewer than two tasks run: tasks stop, or new ones can't start                                                                                                            | read the service's events and a stopped task's `stoppedReason` (`aws ecs describe-tasks`); an image that can't be pulled points at a NAT gateway or ECR      |
| `Items-Read-Throttled-Events-Critical`, `-Write-` | DynamoDB throttled reads or writes: a hot key or a burst past on-demand's limits                                                                                         | find the key in the logs; on-demand adapts within minutes, a hot key needs a change to the key design                                                        |
| `Items-SystemError-Count-Critical`                | DynamoDB failed requests on its side                                                                                                                                     | check the AWS Health Dashboard for DynamoDB in the region; the SDK retries, so watch whether `Service-5xx` fires too                                         |

When an alarm fires, note the time, what you saw and what you did; when it
has fired before for the same cause, that cause needs a fix, not another
note.

## Traces

In production every request but the health checks is traced: the app sends
its spans to the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry collector beside it in the
task, which sends them to X-Ray, under the service name `{{ .Container }}`. A
trace shows the request and each call it made to DynamoDB. With no traces in
X-Ray, read the collector's log group (`CollectorLogs` in the stack): the
collector is not essential, so when it fails the app goes on, untraced. It
has 0.25 vCPU of its own and at most 512 MiB, past which it is stopped, not
the app. A task that stops sends the spans it still holds; when it can't, the
app's log says `The last spans were not sent.` Development traces nothing.

## The network

The tasks reach ECR, CloudWatch Logs and X-Ray through NAT gateways, and
DynamoDB through a gateway endpoint that needs none. Production has a NAT
gateway in each of its two zones: when one zone fails, the tasks in the
other keep their way out, and the load balancer sends every request to
them. Development has one: when its zone fails, no task can pull its image
or write its logs.

## What production costs over development

Every hour, production pays for a second NAT gateway (each charges by the
hour and by the gigabyte it carries) and for tasks twice the size, 0.5 vCPU
and 1 GB instead of 0.25 vCPU and 0.5 GB: the collector beside the app gets
half, so the app has in production what it has in development. X-Ray
charges per trace past its free tier, and every request but the health
checks is traced. Then
point-in-time recovery{{ if ne $ops.Backups.RetentionDays 0 }} and AWS Backup's storage{{ end }} by the table's size{{ if and (ne $ops.Backups.RetentionDays 0) $ops.Backups.CopyToRegion }}, the
copies' transfer to {{ $ops.Backups.CopyToRegion }} and their storage there{{ end }}{{ if ne $ops.Backups.RetentionDays 0 }}, a KMS key per vault{{ end }},
and a year of logs instead of a month. The load balancer, the alarms and
the dashboard cost the same in both tiers.

## What the account provides

The stacks hold only this Container's resources. These are set once per
account, by its owner, and never by a stack here:

- GuardDuty, for threat detection in the account.
- Security Hub, for the account's security findings in one place.
- CloudTrail, recording every API call to an S3 bucket kept apart.
- AWS Config, recording how resources change.
- AWS Budgets, with an alert on what the account spends.
- The cost allocation tags' activation: `c4factory:project`,
  `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container` attribute costs only once the
  account activates them in Billing.
- The ACM certificate the load balancer serves (the `CertificateArn`
  parameter) and the DNS record pointing its domain at `EntryPoint`.

A vault lock on the backup vault is the account owner's call too: in
compliance mode it can never be undone.

